          "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"Ezekiel 27 provides an unusually explicit decoding: Tyre, a city and commercial polity, is constructed as a ship; its inhabitants are mariners, its wise men pilots, its military defenders aboard, its commerce cargo, and the destruction of the vessel is the destruction of Tyre itself. Revelation 18 reuses this maritime lament for Babylon, supporting extension from one city to another organized imperial-commercial body. This evidence is more direct than the traditional Church-as-ship reading, which is recognizable and historically prominent but depends chiefly on typological interpretation of boats and Noah's ark rather than an explicit textual identification. The Church remains a compatible, narrower instance of a governed people under Christ rather than a contradictory alternative. The Gospel evidence also supports the entry's Christological elaboration: Christ walks on and commands the waters without depending on a vessel, while aboard the disciples' vessel His authority governs and preserves its company.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A ship symbolizes an organized kingdom, city, state, or comparable governed body.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"The governed body rides upon a sea representing peoples.\",\n    \"Its rulers steer it.\",\n    \"Its inhabitants crew it.\",\n    \"Christ rules the waters without needing a vessel.\",\n    \"When Christ boards a vessel, it comes under its rightful Head.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The detailed equation of Peter's fear with fear of popular tumult is more speculative than the glossary entry requires.\",\n    \"The Jonah–Caiaphas–Passion correspondence is an extended typological application not necessary to establish the glossary definition.\",\n    \"The claim that Jesus' teaching posture in the boat functions as an enacted throne-parable goes beyond what the Gospel narratives state directly.\",\n    \"The chapter's treatment of every ship as preserving a uniform political structure may require more examination of ordinary narrative uses.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The ship primarily symbolizes the Church, the vessel of salvation carrying believers through the world's dangers under Christ, as traditionally inferred from Noah's ark, the Gospel storm narratives, Peter's boat, and longstanding ecclesial interpretation.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The ship is specifically the Church or ecclesial community of salvation.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_NARROWER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter-reading resists the book's extension of the image to organized polities generally and treats the Church, rather than the broader category of governed bodies, as the ship's primary referent.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"The storm narratives place Christ and the gathered disciples together in a boat preserved through danger.\",\n    \"Luke 5 joins Peter's boat, Christ's teaching, and the mission of gathering people.\",\n    \"Noah's ark and 1 Peter 3:20–21 support a vessel-of-salvation typology.\",\n    \"Patristic interpretation, the Barque of Peter, and the architectural term 'nave' make the ecclesial reading historically prominent.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Ezekiel explicitly identifies the subject as Tyre and maps the city's inhabitants, counselors, military, merchants, and wealth onto the personnel and cargo of one vessel.\",\n    \"It explains why the destruction of the ship is narrated as the destruction of the city itself.\",\n    \"It accounts for Revelation's reuse of Tyre's maritime lament for Babylon, another organized political-commercial power.\",\n    \"It naturally accommodates the Church reading as a governed community under Christ without limiting the image to the Church.\",\n    \"It distinguishes the organized vessel from the individual people aboard, consistent with Acts 27's statement that the ship can perish while its people survive.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It closely fits the gathered disciples' dependence on Christ in the Gospel boat narratives.\",\n    \"It explains the symbol's dominant reception in Christian preaching, art, architecture, and ecclesiology.\",\n    \"It integrates naturally with ark, baptism, and salvation typology.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Ezekiel 27:2–36\",\n    \"Revelation 18:9–19\",\n    \"Isaiah 23:1–18\",\n    \"Matthew 14:22–33\",\n    \"Mark 4:35–41\",\n    \"Acts 27:22, 31, 37, 41–44\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"The evidence establishes the polity meaning most explicitly for Tyre and Babylon; its universal application to every symbolic ship remains an inference.\",\n    \"The identification of the sea specifically as peoples is less directly demonstrated by the excerpts attached to this entry than the identification of the ship as a polity.\",\n    \"The Church-as-ship interpretation remains the historically dominant Christian reading even though its scriptural identifications are less explicit.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"medium\"\n}"
